{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-212672",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x∣y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,3).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(5,5).$\n\nTherefore we have\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(3,3),(5,5)}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
